{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-69d1bc77-920b-49a3-a824-9c1e7f64da62","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b70871b02ecb41a6","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-v496wm9z/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n\nDecomposed from the strategy summary, acceptance criteria, and Staff Engineer Input.\nThe full AI-generated strategy attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) was not\navailable in the workspace, so the Affected Components / Impacted Teams / HLR /\nDependencies / NFR tables could not be read; requirements were derived from the\nstrategy's Acceptance Criteria bullets (see Health Warnings). Where the Staff\nEngineer Input diverges from the AI-generated strategy, the Staff Engineer Input\nwas followed (Step 1.5); each override is logged below.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Provisioning approach go/no-go for the RHOAI 3.5 default vector store | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable and validate the pgvector remote vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Wire Gen AI Studio playground RAG to the pgvector vector_io provider | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and finalize default resource sizing | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E005 | Operator-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E005 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for Gen AI Studio playground RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n\nMain plan: E001–E004. Branch epics BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 are conditional alternatives\nselected by the outcome of E001; exactly one is expected to execute.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001 --> BRANCH_A_E005\n    E001 --> BRANCH_B_E005\n    E001 --> BRANCH_C_E005\n    E002 --> E003\n    E002 --> E004\n```\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent. E001 and E002 are roots. Main-plan\ncritical path length: 2 (E002 → E003). Branch epics are excluded from the\nmain-plan critical path.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The go/no-go decision determines which provisioning epic exists. True gate: `remove` — each branch epic only exists if its outcome is selected. Bounded outcomes (3) → conditional branches rather than phased decomposition. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The gen-ai BFF's provider wiring builds against the OGX distribution's pgvector vector_io provider configuration surface; the provider must be enabled and validated first. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The benchmark measures the pgvector provider integration; it requires a working provider to measure. |\n| E001 ∥ E002, E003, E004 (no edges) | Rule 17 / Step 5.5 (tiered delivery) | Provider enablement, BFF wiring, and benchmarking deliver partial value independent of the provisioning decision and of RHAISTRAT-7164 timing. Wiring consumes a connection secret regardless of who provisions the database, so it is not gated by the decision. |\n| Branch epics ∥ E002/E003/E004 (no edges) | Branch rules | Branch epic dependencies carry only the gating Investigation. Integration readiness (provider validated, wiring present) is expressed as acceptance criteria on the branch epics, not DAG edges. |\n\nNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are\ngenerated: Staff Engineer Input establishes that the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL\n16 image already includes pgvector and is already referenced for disconnected\ninstalls, so no custom image build or pipeline onboarding is needed (Rule 20\nonboarding chain not triggered — the image is existing platform inventory,\nprovisioning follows the model-registry-operator pattern).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nThe strategy's HLR table was unavailable (attachment missing); requirement IDs\nbelow are derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria bullets. The strategy\nfrontmatter priority is Blocker, so all in-scope requirements are treated as P0\nexcept the sizing validation (AC-6), scored P1 as a validation deliverable rather\nthan user-facing capability. No priority-split (bundling) analysis applies: no\nrequirement pair shares a surface at different priority labels.\n\n| Requirement (derived) | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----------------------|----------|---------|-------|\n| AC-1: Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration of external infrastructure | P0 | E003, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 | BRANCH-C (bring-your-own) explicitly does not satisfy the out-of-the-box clause — it is the recorded fallback if neither provisioning approach is viable; flagged in Health Warnings. |\n| AC-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability | P0 | E002, E003, BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Capability parity is measured order-of-magnitude vs the inline Milvus baseline (Staff Engineer Input: dev-experimentation scope, no SLA thresholds) — validated by E004. |\n| AC-3: Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | — | (none) | **Removed from scope per Staff Engineer Input**: Auto Rag integration targets RHOAI 3.6 under RHAISTRAT-7824; this strategy validates pgvector for Gen AI Studio Playground only. Logged as override O-3 below. |\n| AC-4: Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX/Llama Stack instance (not inline) | P0 | E002, BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Remote pgvector provider, separate PostgreSQL process. |\n| AC-5: Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | P0 | BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Per-namespace scoping is the preferred model (Silas Brenmark); the per-instance counter-consideration existed only for Auto Rag, which is decoupled. |\n| AC-6: Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments | P1 | E004, BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Sizing starts from model-registry-operator PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjusted per E004 benchmark findings. Flat scan (no index) is sufficient for typical playground document sets. |\n\nOpen questions from the strategy:\n- *Scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance)* — resolved by Staff Engineer\n  Input (per-namespace preferred; the per-instance need was Auto Rag's, now\n  decoupled). Captured as scope on the branch epics, not an Investigation.\n- *Migration path to a production-grade vector DB* — does not change which epics\n  exist; captured as an acceptance criterion on E003 (standard OGX vector_io\n  provider settings so the connection can be redirected to an external vector\n  database without BFF changes).\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\n| # | Override | Effect on decomposition |\n|---|----------|------------------------|\n| O-1 | OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL; it defaults to SQLite (`storage.kv.type`/`storage.sql.type: sqlite`), and the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite. PostgreSQL for pgvector is new work, not an extension of existing infrastructure. | Motivates the provisioning epics (branch epics) as new work; no \"extend existing PG\" epic. |\n| O-2 | A Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image with pgvector already exists (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`); model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it; related-image env var already in the rhods-operator CSV.