{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b611c5abbb7b781c","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: ogx-distribution packages the OGX framework as a containerized Python service for RHOAI. It acts as a unified AI agent gateway that routes inference requests to configurable remote providers (vLLM, AWS Bedrock, IBM WatsonX, Azure, Vertex AI, OpenAI, Gemini, Anthropic), provides vector I/O through Milvus, PGVector, or Qdrant backends, and offers tool runtime capabilities including web search and file operations. The distribution uses PostgreSQL for both key-value and SQL-backed persistence of agent state, conversations, batches, and metadata. Authentication is handled via OAuth2 token validation with JWKS when configured. The container image is built on an AIPCC base image with Python dependencies installed from the RHEL AI Python Package Index via Hermeto-prefetched packages, and ships pre-fetched Docling and embedding model artifacts. [source: distribution/config.yaml:1-280, Dockerfile.konflux:1-58, distribution/entrypoint.sh:1-79]\n20:OGX distribution follows a provider-plugin architecture where each API capability (inference, vector I/O, tool runtime, files, batches, responses, messages) is backed by one or more configurable providers. Providers are conditionally activated via environment variable guards (e.g., `${env.VLLM_URL:+vllm-inference}`), meaning the running configuration adapts to the deployment context without code changes. The distribution strips several inline providers (docling, faiss, milvus-inline, sentence-transformers) from the upstream build configuration, shipping only remote-capable providers suitable for production multi-worker deployments. [source: distribution/config.yaml:4-12, build/build.yaml:1-5]\n26:Storage is centralized on PostgreSQL with two backend types: `kv_postgres` for key-value stores (registry metadata, agent state, vector store namespaces) and `sql_postgres` for relational data (inference logs, conversations, batches, file metadata, connectors). This dual-backend pattern allows the framework to use the appropriate access pattern for each data type while sharing a single database instance. [source: distribution/config.yaml:198-235]\n69:| /v1/vector-io/* | ALL | 8321/TCP | HTTP | HTTP/1.1 | None (platform-delegated) | OAuth2 token (when configured) | ogx-distribution | Vector database operations |\n90:| PostgreSQL | SQL/KV client | Required | Key-value and relational storage for agent state, conversations, batches, metadata, inference logs, and vector store metadata |\n148:| **Certificate handling** | System trust store (inherited from UBI base); configurable CA paths for Milvus (`ca_pem_path`, `client_pem_path`) and auth (`tls_cafile`) | distribution/config.yaml:89-91,268 |\n167:| 2 | OGX Server | Vector DB (Milvus / PGVector / Qdrant) | Configurable | HTTP/gRPC/SQL | Configurable (mTLS for Milvus) | Token / API key / password |\n186:| Milvus | HTTP client | Optional | Configurable | HTTP/HTTPS | mTLS configurable | Remote vector database for RAG |\n187:| PGVector | SQL client | Optional | 5432/TCP | PostgreSQL | Configurable | Vector database via PostgreSQL extension |\n188:| Qdrant | HTTP/gRPC client | Optional | 6333/TCP (HTTP), 6334/TCP (gRPC) | HTTP or gRPC | HTTPS configurable | Vector database for RAG |\n---\n16:**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n20:The odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n79:| gen-ai BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated gen-ai backend-for-frontend service |\n97:| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n98:| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n99:| packages/gen-ai/bff/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n151:| /gen-ai/api/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n152:| /gen-ai/api/v1/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n198:| github.com/ogx-ai/ogx-k8s-operator | v0.9.0 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n299:| Llama Stack Server | varies | HTTPS | TLS | Bearer Token / API Key | LLM inference, vector stores, and files |\n453:| /gen-ai/api/v1/* | ALL | Bearer Token (x-forwarded-access-token) | Go BFF middleware (RequireAccessToService) | RBAC and namespace access |\n506:- **BFF sidecar traffic:** Each federated module's API calls are routed by path prefix to the corresponding Go BFF sidecar (e.g., `/gen-ai/api/*` → port 8143, `/model-registry/api/*` → port 8043, `/agent-ops/api/*` → port 8843). BFF sidecars authenticate requests independently using Bearer tokens, perform SubjectAccessReviews for authorization, and issue Kubernetes API calls using either the user's forwarded token or an internal ServiceAccount token, depending on the configured `auth-method` flag. Two Kubernetes Service objects expose the operator's metrics (port 8443) and webhook (port 443→9443) endpoints internally. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, packages/gen-ai/bff/internal/constants/api_constants.go:15, dashboard-operator/config/webhook/manifests.yaml:29]\n508:- **External service interactions:** BFF sidecars proxy requests to external platform services: gen-ai BFF connects to Llama Stack servers, MLflow tracking servers, NeMo guardrails, and MCP servers (via SSE/streamable HTTP with `X-MCP-Bearer` authentication); eval-hub BFF queries TrustyAI and KServe InferenceServices; maas BFF communicates with MaaS API servers; and the core-bff proxies to Prometheus/Thanos for metrics dashboards. All external connections use TLS encryption with Bearer token or API key authentication. [source: packages/gen-ai/bff/internal/constants/mcp.go:5-16, packages/eval-hub/bff/internal/api/inferenceservices_handler.go:94]\n548:| Llama Stack Server | REST |  | varies | HTTPS | TLS | LLM inference, vector stores, and files |\n---\n14:**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of ModelRegistry, Catalog, and AIHub custom resources, provisioning model-registry service instances with kube-rbac-proxy authentication, PostgreSQL storage, and Gateway API or OpenShift Route ingress. [source: cmd/modelregistry.go:169, internal/controller/modelregistry_controller.go:282, internal/controller/modelcatalog_controller.go:183]\n16:**Detailed**: The model-registry-operator is a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles three CRDs — ModelRegistry (v1alpha1/v1beta1), Catalog (v1alpha1), and AIHub (v1alpha1) — to provision and manage model registry service instances within the RHOAI platform. For each ModelRegistry CR, the operator creates a Deployment running the model-registry proxy backed by either PostgreSQL or MySQL storage, with an optional kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that enforces SubjectAccessReview-based authorization on the HTTPS port. The operator manages the full resource lifecycle including Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC Roles/Bindings, Secrets, NetworkPolicies, and ingress resources (Gateway API HTTPRoutes or OpenShift Routes depending on cluster capabilities). The ModelCatalog reconciler similarly provisions a centralized catalog service with its own PostgreSQL instance. The operator detects cluster capabilities at startup — including OpenShift APIs, BYOIDC mode (where user.openshift.io is absent), Gateway API availability, and the platform Auth CRD — to conditionally create platform-specific resources. [source: internal/controller/util.go:36-39, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:197-211, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\n53:| model-catalog-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgresql ({image}) |\n57:| {registry-name}-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgres ({image}) |\n137:| model-catalog-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n141:| {registry-name}-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n243:| Secret Name | Type | Purpose | Provisioned By | Auto-Rotate |\n246:| model-catalog-postgres | Opaque | model-catalog/catalog, model-catalog-postgres/postgresql | controller template | Unknown |\n248:| {registry-name}-postgres-credentials | Opaque | {registry-name}-postgres/postgres | controller Go source | Unknown |\n250:| {name}-postgres | Opaque |  | controller Go source | Unknown |\n302:- **Data storage:** Each model-registry instance connects to a PostgreSQL or MySQL database with configurable connection parameters including SSL/TLS certificates, keys, and root CA bundles mounted from Secrets and ConfigMaps. Database credentials are injected via environment variables from referenced Secrets. [source: internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:40-68, 107-131]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84165dc2c6681480","agent_id":"a64440399190e4b93","session_id":"6460c734-9da5-4d64-9956-6c822c41e6b0","uuid":"085dae1f-593b-4366-9ab7-1c5a0fe97bfc","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:15:50.661Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b611c5abbb7b781c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
