venance),\nwhich the epic content bears out.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nComponent/team tuples separate cleanly: E002 is the only epic on OGX Core; all\nother epics are Gen AI Studio / Gen AI Studio. Team assignment for the OGX Core\nwork is inferred (documented in Health Warning 1) and consistent with the Staff\nEngineer Input's \"validate pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground.\" No\nscope duplication: E002 (provider enablement in the distribution), E003 (BFF\nwiring), E004 (benchmark/sizing), and the branch epics (provisioning) touch\ndistinct surfaces; the end-to-end validation ACs repeated across E003 and the\nbranch epics are composite integration checks over different deliverables\n(wiring vs provisioning), not independent rebuilds of the same surface. No\nwork-product type mixing — BRANCH-C's customer configuration documentation is\nincidental reference material falling out of the change and explicitly scoped as\n\"no separate content work\"; E004's report is the deliverable of an engineering\nbenchmark, not content authoring. E003's size (one logical change spanning all\nthree provisioning outcomes) already carries a concrete re-split trigger in\nHealth Warning 6 (branch-specific code-path divergence) — a managed watch item,\nnot a defect.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome determines which of three branch\nepics exists (gate_failure_impact action `remove` on all three branches), so it\nchanges downstream structure, not just scheduling. Every gated epic has both\n`action` and `fallback_approach` populated with substantive fallbacks. Every\n`gated_by` target (E001) appears in the gating epic's direct `dependencies`.\nNo epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies` without `gated_by` (only the\nbranch epics reference E001, and all three set `gated_by`). All Implementation\nepics produce concrete artifacts (provider configuration + validation, BFF code\nchanges, benchmark report + sizing recommendation, provisioning code). E004 is\ncorrectly typed Implementation: it produces a harness, report, and sizing\ndeliverable and its outcome does not change which epics exist. Signal fields\nare type-appropriate everywhere (investigation_signals on E001 only; ai_signals\non all Implementation epics).\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nSignal values are consistent with the type-appropriate rubrics and the strategy\ncontent. E001's `source_accessibility: 0` correctly reflects that the gating\nanswer is a pending delivery/human decision (the exact miscalibration the\nrubric warns about would be scoring it 1), and `human_judgment_required: -2`\nmatches a sprint-planning go/no-go. E003's `open_questions: 0` matches its own\n\"partially resolved\" rationale (secret shape depends on the selected branch);\nBRANCH-B's `open_questions: -1` matches the Staff-flagged lifecycle design\nquestions; BRANCH-A's `external_dependency: -1` matches the hard\nRHAISTRAT-7164 dependency. Frontmatter values match the ai-signals files\nline-for-line. Architecture-claims calibration was verified against\n`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/`: ogx-distribution.md\ncorroborates remote Milvus/PGVector/Qdrant vector I/O with env-guarded\nactivation and stripped inline providers (E002); odh-dashboard.md corroborates\nthe gen-ai BFF sidecar and its Llama Stack/OGX instance creation (E003,\nBRANCH-B); model-registry-operator.md corroborates controller-created postgres\nDeployments/Services/credential secrets (E004, BRANCH-A); ogx-k8s-operator.md\ncorroborates the OGXServer CR surface (BRANCH-C). One nit: BRANCH-A's\narchitecture_claims rationale credits model-registry-operator.md with\ncorroborating \"the postgresql-16 image use,\" but that file shows an unspecified\n`postgres ({image})` — the image fact rests on Staff Engineer Input's own\ncitations (sclorg Dockerfile, rhods-operator CSV). The signal value (1) is\nstill correct; only the rationale's attribution is imprecise. Per instructions,\nai_implementability labels/scores were treated as pipeline-computed and not\naudited.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs on its own deliverables. No rule-mandated AC is\nmissing: there are no konflux-chain epics (Rule 25 n/a), no docs-authoring\nepics (Rule 26 n/a), and no replacement-of-live-behavior epic — the inline\nprovider removal is RHAISTRAT-7474's scope; this epic set is additive, and\nE002's env-guarded activation is itself the toggle. No externally-controlled\nprecondition is misfiled as an AC — the branch epics' ACs condition on what\nthey build/provision, E001's socialization AC is the epic's own action, and\nE004's order-of-magnitude comparison is measurable. The RHAISTRAT-7164\nschedule risk is correctly placed in Risks/Health Warnings (HW 2), not as an\nAC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID (grep-verified — scope\nand ACs say \"the benchmark findings,\" not \"E004\").\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll in-scope strategy capabilities are accounted for: out-of-the-box default\nvector store (E002/E003 + branches A/B), separate-process requirement (E002 +\nbranches), multi-tenant scoping (branches), lightweight sizing (E004), with\nAuto Rag's removal justified, documented as override O-3, and surfaced as a\ncommunication risk in Health Warning 3. Strategy-level context is carried\nforward, not dropped: both open questions are resolved and their resolution\nlocated (scoping model → branch epic scope; migration path → E003 AC on\nstandard OGX provider settings); the AC-2 parity-vs-dev-experimentation tension\nis surfaced in Health Warning 4; the RHAISTRAT-7164 release-commitment risk in\nHealth Warning 2. Conditional branches cover all three bounded outcomes named\nin the Staff Engineer Input (operator-managed, BFF-managed, bring-your-own),\nwith branch C's out-of-the-box degradation explicitly recorded rather than\nsilently accepted. One unverifiable item: the strategy's Prerequisites &