er\n39\tInput override logged (O-1 through O-6). This is a transparent, documented data\n40\tgap, not a coverage failure: a traceability matrix exists and every derived\n41\trequirement maps to at least one epic.\n42\t\n43\t## Criterion Details\n44\t\n45\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n46\tThe strategy's HLR table was unavailable (attachment missing), so the\n47\tdecomposition synthesized AC-1..AC-6 from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria\n48\tbullets and documented the derivation. All six map to epics: AC-1 → E003 +\n49\tBRANCH-A/B-E005 (BRANCH-C's non-satisfaction of the out-of-the-box clause is\n50\texplicitly recorded in the matrix row and Health Warning 5); AC-2 → E002/E003/\n51\tE004 + branches; AC-3 (Auto Rag) removed from scope with justification per Staff\n52\tEngineer Input (RHOAI 3.6, RHAISTRAT-7824) as override O-3; AC-4 → E002 +\n53\tbranches; AC-5 → branches; AC-6 → E004 + branches. Priority inheritance is\n54\tsound: E004 (P1) blocks nothing; no P0 epic depends on lower-priority work; no\n55\tdocs-authoring epics exist so the exemption is moot (validator confirmed).\n56\tPriority collapse: the substantive P1 work (sizing validation) is separately\n57\tscheduled in E004 at P1. The branch epics (P0) carry AC-6 only incidentally —\n58\tresource requests/limits are inherent to creating the provisioned Deployment,\n59\tnot a deferrable feature — so no bundling analysis is required. The summary's\n60\tclaim that \"no requirement pair shares a surface at different priority labels\"\n61\tis slightly imprecise (branch epics do list AC-6 alongside P0 HLRs), but the\n62\tsubstance is correct and nothing deferrable is bundled.\n63\t\n64\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n65\tDiagram and frontmatter match exactly: E001→BRANCH-A/B/C-E005, E002→E003,\n66\tE002→E004; E001 and E002 are roots; arrows run dependency→dependent. No\n67\tcircular dependencies, no edges to nonexistent epics. All three branch files set\n68\tboth `branch` (A/B/C, matching filename) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001`,\n69\tand E001 appears in each branch's direct `dependencies`. No main-plan epic\n70\tlists a branch epic in its dependencies — the branch-consumption rules are\n71\tsatisfied. Critical path length 2 (E002→E003) is within the S/M heuristic\n72\t(1-2/3-4). Parallelism: E003 and E004 run in parallel after E002; the branch\n73\tepics and the Tier-1 implementation epics are deliberately unconnected, with\n74\tthe integration coupling (provider validated, wiring present) expressed as\n75\tacceptance criteria on the branch epics and the rationale recorded in the DAG\n76\tJustification table — the branch epics' own deliverable (provisioning) consumes\n77\tno E002/E003 artifact, so this AC-gate shape does not hide a data dependency.\n78\tE001∥E002/E003/E004 is justified by tiered delivery: E003's wiring is\n79\tprovenance-agnostic (consumes a connection secret regardless of provenance),\n80\twhich the epic content bears out.\n81\t\n82\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n83\tComponent/team tuples separate cleanly: E002 is the only epic on OGX Core; all\n84\tother epics are Gen AI Studio / Gen AI Studio. Team assignment for the OGX Core\n85\twork is inferred (documented in Health Warning 1) and consistent with the Staff\n86\tEngineer Input's \"validate pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground.\" No\n87\tscope duplication: E002 (provider enablement in the distribution), E003 (BFF\n88\twiring), E004 (benchmark/sizing), and the branch epics (provisioning) touch\n89\tdistinct surfaces; the end-to-end validation ACs repeated across E003 and the\n90\tbranch epics are composite integration checks over different deliverables\n91\t(wiring vs provisioning), not independent rebuilds of the same surface. No\n92\twork-product type mixing — BRANCH-C's customer configuration documentation is\n93\tincidental reference material falling out of the change and explicitly scoped as\n94\t\"no separate content work\"; E004's report is the deliverable of an engineering\n95\tbenchmark, n