{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9002b3628aac9606","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8004 (Default Vector Store for Gen AI Studio)\n11\t\n12\tDecomposed from the strategy summary, acceptance criteria, and Staff Engineer Input.\n13\tThe full AI-generated strategy attachment (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) was not\n14\tavailable in the workspace, so the Affected Components / Impacted Teams / HLR /\n15\tDependencies / NFR tables could not be read; requirements were derived from the\n16\tstrategy's Acceptance Criteria bullets (see Health Warnings). Where the Staff\n17\tEngineer Input diverges from the AI-generated strategy, the Staff Engineer Input\n18\twas followed (Step 1.5); each override is logged below.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E001 | Provisioning approach go/no-go for the RHOAI 3.5 default vector store | Investigation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 | Enable and validate the pgvector remote vector_io provider in the OGX distribution | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Wire Gen AI Studio playground RAG to the pgvector vector_io provider | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-E004 | Benchmark pgvector against the inline Milvus baseline and finalize default resource sizing | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E005 | Operator-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E005 | BFF-managed PostgreSQL provisioning for the Gen AI Studio default vector store | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-C-E005 | Bring-your-own PostgreSQL support for Gen AI Studio playground RAG | Implementation | Gen AI Studio | P0 |\n31\t\n32\tMain plan: E001–E004. Branch epics BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 are conditional alternatives\n33\tselected by the outcome of E001; exactly one is expected to execute.\n34\t\n35\t## Dependency DAG\n36\t\n37\t```mermaid\n38\tgraph TD\n39\t    E001 --> BRANCH_A_E005\n40\t    E001 --> BRANCH_B_E005\n41\t    E001 --> BRANCH_C_E005\n42\t    E002 --> E003\n43\t    E002 --> E004\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\tArrows point from dependency to dependent. E001 and E002 are roots. Main-plan\n47\tcritical path length: 2 (E002 → E003). Branch epics are excluded from the\n48\tmain-plan critical path.\n49\t\n50\t## DAG Justification\n51\t\n52\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n53\t|------|------|-----------|\n54\t| E001 → BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The go/no-go decision determines which provisioning epic exists. True gate: `remove` — each branch epic only exists if its outcome is selected. Bounded outcomes (3) → conditional branches rather than phased decomposition. |\n55\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The gen-ai BFF's provider wiring builds against the OGX distribution's pgvector vector_io provider configuration surface; the provider must be enabled and validated first. |\n56\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The benchmark measures the pgvector provider integration; it requires a working provider to measure. |\n57\t| E001 ∥ E002, E003, E004 (no edges) | Rule 17 / Step 5.5 (tiered delivery) | Provider enablement, BFF wiring, and benchmarking deliver partial value independent of the provisioning decision and of RHAISTRAT-7164 timing. Wiring consumes a connection secret regardless of who provisions the database, so it is not gated by the decision. |\n58\t| Branch epics ∥ E002/E003/E004 (no edges) | Branch rules | Branch epic dependencies carry only the gating Investigation. Integration readiness (provider validated, wiring present) is expressed as acceptance criteria on the branch epics, not DAG edges. |\n59\t\n60\tNo `konflux-onboarding`, `repo-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are\n61\tgenerated: Staff Engineer Input establishes that the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL\n62\t16 image already includes pgvector and is already referenced for disconnected\n63\tinstalls, so no custom image build or pipeline onboarding is needed (Rule 20\n64\tonboarding chain not triggered — the image is existing platform inventory,\n65\tprovisioning follows the model-registry-operator pattern).\n66\t\n67\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n68\t\n69\tThe strategy's HLR table was unavailable (attachment missing); requirement IDs\n70\tbelow are derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria bullets. The strategy\n71\tfrontmatter priority is Blocker, so all in-scope requirements are treated as P0\n72\texcept the sizing validation (AC-6), scored P1 as a validation deliverable rather\n73\tthan user-facing capability. No priority-split (bundling) analysis applies: no\n74\trequirement pair shares a surface at different priority labels.\n75\t\n76\t| Requirement (derived) | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n77\t|-----------------------|----------|---------|-------|\n78\t| AC-1: Default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning/configuration of external infrastructure | P0 | E003, BRANCH-A-E005, BRANCH-B-E005 | BRANCH-C (bring-your-own) explicitly does not satisfy the out-of-the-box clause — it is the recorded fallback if neither provisioning approach is viable; flagged in Health Warnings. |\n79\t| AC-2: Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of current capability | P0 | E002, E003, BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Capability parity is measured order-of-magnitude vs the inline Milvus baseline (Staff Engineer Input: dev-experimentation scope, no SLA thresholds) — validated by E004. |\n80\t| AC-3: Auto Rag functions with the default vector store | — | (none) | **Removed from scope per Staff Engineer Input**: Auto Rag integration targets RHOAI 3.6 under RHAISTRAT-7824; this strategy validates pgvector for Gen AI Studio Playground only. Logged as override O-3 below. |\n81\t| AC-4: Default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX/Llama Stack instance (not inline) | P0 | E002, BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Remote pgvector provider, separate PostgreSQL process. |\n82\t| AC-5: Scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | P0 | BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Per-namespace scoping is the preferred model (Silas Brenmark); the per-instance counter-consideration existed only for Auto Rag, which is decoupled. |\n83\t| AC-6: Lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments | P1 | E004, BRANCH-A/B/C-E005 | Sizing starts from model-registry-operator PostgreSQL requests/limits, adjusted per E004 benchmark findings. Flat scan (no index) is sufficient for typical playground document sets. |\n84\t\n85\tOpen questions from the strategy:\n86\t- *Scoping model (per-namespace vs per-instance)* — resolved by Staff Engineer\n87\t  Input (per-namespace preferred; the per-instance need was Auto Rag's, now\n88\t  decoupled). Captured as scope on the branch epics, not an Investigation.\n89\t- *Migration path to a production-grade vector DB* — does not change which epics\n90\t  exist; captured as an acceptance criterion on E003 (standard OGX vector_io\n91\t  provider settings so the connection can be redirected to an external vector\n92\t  database without BFF changes).\n93\t\n94\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n95\t\n96\t| # | Override | Effect on decomposition |\n97\t|---|----------|------------------------|\n98\t| O-1 | OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL; it defaults to SQLite (`storage.kv.type`/`storage.sql.type: sqlite`), and the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite. PostgreSQL for pgvector is new work, not an extension of existing infrastructure. | Motivates the provisioning epics (branch epics) as new work; no \"extend existing PG\" epic. |\n99\t| O-2 | A Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image with pgvector already exists (`registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16`); model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it; related-image env var already in the rhods-operator CSV. | Eliminates the AI strategy's image-build/onboarding epic and any Konflux onboarding epic (1–2 sprint estimate removed). Branch-A provisions the existing image only. |\n100\t| O-3 | Decouple from Auto Rag (RHOAI 3.6, RHAISTRAT-7824); validate pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG. | Removes Auto Rag from affected components, acceptance criteria (AC-3), and validation scope. |\n101\t| O-4 | Performance is dev-experimentation scope: no latency SLAs; sprint-1 benchmark verifies same order of magnitude vs inline Milvus baseline; flat scan sufficient. | E004 ACs use order-of-magnitude comparison, not thresholds; no latency/index-selection Investigation epic. |\n102\t| O-5 | Vector storage (vector_io `remote::pgvector` via `ENABLE_PGVECTOR`) and application storage (SQLite) are independent configurations; no consolidation, no SQLite→PostgreSQL migration. | E002/E003 scope explicitly excludes storage migration; no migration epic. |\n103\t| O-6 | Operator dependency RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization, In Progress) is a hard dependency for operator-managed self-provisioning; go/no-go gate required; fallbacks: BFF-managed provisioning or bring-your-own PostgreSQL. | E001 Investigation with three bounded outcomes → conditional branch epics A/B/C; tiered delivery so provider work proceeds independently. |\n104\t\n105\t## Health Warnings\n106\t\n107\t1. **Missing strategy attachment (non-blocking, data gap):** the full strategy\n108\t   (`RHAISTRAT-8004-strategy.md`) was not available, so the Affected Components,\n109\t   Impacted Teams, HLR, Dependencies, and NFR tables could not be parsed. The\n110\t   decomposition is derived from the Summary, Acceptance Criteria, and Staff\n111\t   Engineer Input. Requirement IDs are synthesized from AC bullets; team\n112\t   assignment (Gen AI Studio for all epics, including the OGX Core component\n113\t   work, consistent with \"validate pgvector only for Gen AI Studio Playground\")\n114\t   is inferred from the strategy labels (`dashboard-crimson-scrum`, `playground`)\n115\t   and should be verified against the full strategy. **Documentation Support\n116\t   check (revision):** the strategy content available in the workspace contains\n117\t   no Prerequisites & Process Gates section and no `Documentation Support` entry\n118\t   (the full attachment remains unavailable), so no Rule 11 docs-authoring epic\n119\t   is generated — nothing in the available data establishes documentation scope.\n120\t   If the full attachment later lists `Documentation Support: Yes`, a\n121\t   docs-authoring epic blocked by the implementation epics must be added at\n122\t   that point.\n123\t2. **Hard external dependency with uncertain timing (schedule risk):**\n124\t   RHAISTRAT-7164 (dashboard operator modularization) is In Progress and gates\n125\t   operator-managed provisioning. Staff: \"It will be challenging to commit this\n126\t   strategy to a specific release until that dependency is delivered.\" The E001\n127\t   go/no-go gate with pre-analyzed fallbacks (branches B/C) is the mitigation;\n128\t   the release-commitment risk itself remains and is externally controlled.\n129\t3. **Scope reduction vs strategy text:** the strategy Summary and Success\n130\t   Criteria still name Auto Rag; Staff Engineer Input removes it (RHOAI 3.6,\n131\t   RHAISTRAT-7824). Stakeholder communication is needed so Auto Rag consumers\n132\t   are not surprised — RHAISTRAT-7474 (inline provider removal) remains blocked\n133\t   only on the Gen AI Studio scope delivered here.\n134\t4. **AC tension:** \"no loss of current capability\" (AC-2) reads as strict parity,\n135\t   while Staff Engineer Input scopes performance to dev experimentation\n136\t   (order-of-magnitude, flat scan, no index). E004's ACs follow the Staff\n137\t   Engineer Input; reviewers should confirm product accepts this framing.\n138\t5. **Branch C does not satisfy AC-1** (out-of-the-box): bring-your-own\n139\t   PostgreSQL is explicitly a fallback that degrades the out-of-the-box\n140\t   experience, aligned with OGX 1.0 breaking changes. Selecting branch C is a\n141\t   product decision, recorded in E001's decision record.\n142\t6. **Re-split trigger (E003):** the BFF wiring epic is kept as one logical change\n143\t   (single pass over the OGXServer instance-creation code path) even though it\n144\t   spans all three provisioning outcomes. Re-split if the wiring grows beyond a\n145\t   single mechanical pass over that surface — e.g., if per-branch wiring\n146\t   divergence (user-supplied vs provisioned connection handling) forces\n147\t   branch-specific code paths, move the divergent parts into the branch epics.\n148\t\n149\t## Tiered Delivery\n150\t\n151\tExternal dependency RHAISTRAT-7164 has uncertain timing; partial value is\n152\tdeliverable without it (Step 5.5):\n153\t\n154\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately):** E002 (pgvector provider enabled\n155\t  and validated in the OGX distribution), E003 (BFF provider wiring — consumes a\n156\t  connection secret regardless of provenance), E004 (baseline benchmark and\n157\t  sizing). These de-risk the vector-store integration and are reusable under\n158\t  every provisioning outcome, including a future platform-provided shared\n159\t  PostgreSQL (RHOAI 3.6).\n160\t- **Tier 2 (gated by E001 / RHAISTRAT-7164 timing):** BRANCH-A-E005\n161\t  (operator-managed provisioning), BRANCH-B-E005 (BFF-managed provisioning),\n162\t  BRANCH-C-E005 (bring-your-own fallback). Exactly one branch executes based on\n163\t  the go/no-go outcome.\n164\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-831cbe92923c654c","agent_id":"abdd64eda1daf9810","session_id":"6460c734-9d